  "summary": "Photocopier as press: glue, toner noise and 40 copies of the truth.",
  "description": "The zine — cut-and-paste punk's native press (Sniffin' Glue 1976, Slash, Maximumrocknroll) — built a 50-year aesthetic from the photocopier's flaws: degraded contrast, margin loss, crooked pasted letters, correction-fluid strokes, screentone photo crashes and hand-numbered editions. Riot grrrl (Bikini Kill, 1990s) turned the form feminist-punk canon; perzines, queerzines and fanzines kept it personal. The look — xerox noise as texture, ransom-note typography, band photo halftones — is now a deliberate style (every \"DIY\" brand poster), while zine libraries (Barnard Zine Library, Salford Zine Library) archive the originals as material culture of pre-internet networks.",
  "culturalContext": "The zine is DIY culture's native press: punk's cut-and-paste built a 50-year aesthetic of toner noise and ransom-note type, riot grrrl made it feminist canon, and zine libraries archive the photocopier as the pre-internet network's printing press.",
